kind of topics that we're talking about, right? And so that's the same problem that the AI system is trying to solve. So it's kind of like trying to build up, just based on the experience that it has from language, a model of the world that's good enough to help it to make that prediction of what word is going to be. And so the problem of predicting the next word is not one that sort of, you know, sets you up to think about whether that next thing is actually true or not, right? So now if you ask this system, okay, here's some text, what's a likely response to this text, it's going to generate something, which is a sort of high probability response based on all of the knowledge that it has about language, but it's not going to be saying, okay, is this true

14
00:08:37.000 --> 00:09:13.000
or not, whereas a human being that you're interacting with and you're sort of having a conversation with might be thinking about whether things are true or false. So the chatbot, unlike human beings, is not willfully lying. It's just making a selection based on what's available to it. Yeah, it's not even thinking about truth or falsehood. It's just thinking about what's a plausible answer to this question. Now, in a recent story in APA's magazine, the Monitor, you said, quote, a lot of what's driving progress is the capacities that these AI systems have, and that's outstripping how well we understand how they work. That's directly quoted from you.

